; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Down and Back Up
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     recursion, call stack, order, staircase
; SUMMARY:  Shows that code after a recursive call runs on the way back up, with a printout and a staircase the turtle climbs down and up.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   DOWNUP prints a word, calls itself with the word one letter
;   shorter, and then prints the word AGAIN. The second print has to
;   wait until every deeper call has finished, so the words come out
;   shrinking and then growing:  hello ello llo lo o lo llo ello hello.
;   STAIRS does the same with the turtle: it walks down a step in blue
;   before the recursive call, and back up a step in red after it.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and look at the printout and the drawing.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the SECOND print in DOWNUP, the line just
;      after  downup butfirst :w  and run with F5.
;      Look at the Call Stack: it is as tall as it will ever get. The
;      smallest word, o, is about to be printed the second time.
;   3. Press F5 again and watch the stack shrink by one each time.
;   4. Slide the speed slider to the left and run again to watch the
;      turtle go down the blue steps and up the red ones.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * instructions before the recursive call run on the way down
;   * instructions after it run on the way back up, in reverse order
;   * every unfinished call is remembered on the call stack
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Delete the second print. Then delete the first one instead.
;   * Make STAIRS turn a little at each step to make a spiral stair.
;   * Change the starting word to your name.
; ============================================================

to downup :w
  print :w
  if emptyp butfirst :w [stop]
  downup butfirst :w
  print :w
end

; :n steps to go. Each step is drawn in blue on the way down; the
; matching step back up is drawn in red, slightly to one side so you
; can see both paths.
to stairs :n
  if :n = 0 [
    ; the bottom of the stairs: mark it with a yellow dot
    setpencolor 6
    filled 6 [repeat 12 [fd 3 rt 30]]
    stop
  ]
  setpencolor 1
  rt 90 fd 50 rt 90 fd 35 lt 180
  stairs :n - 1
  setpencolor 4
  ; climb back a little above and to the right of the blue path
  penup rt 90 fd 5 lt 90 fd 5 pendown
  fd 35 lt 90 fd 50 lt 90
  ; now facing down: turn to face up again, and step back to the
  ; left, so the caller finds the turtle exactly where it left it
  rt 180
  penup bk 5 lt 90 fd 5 rt 90 pendown
end

print [The word goes down and comes back up:]
downup "hello
clearscreen hideturtle
setpensize 3
penup setxy -175 130 setheading 0 pendown
stairs 7
penup setxy -200 170 setpencolor 0
label [Blue going down, red coming back up]
